              "sections": [
                {
                  "heading": "Academic Lecture Notes & Analytical Framework",
                  "urduHeading": "علمی نوٹس اور فکری جائزہ",
                  "bodyEn": "Topology investigates the invariant properties of geometric spaces that remain preserved under continuous deformations (stretching, twisting, bending without tearing or gluing).\n\nMetric Spaces (M, d):\nA set M equipped with a distance function d: M × M -> ℝ satisfying:\n1. d(x, y) ≥ 0, and d(x, y) = 0 if and only if x = y.\n2. Symmetry: d(x, y) = d(y, x).\n3. Triangle Inequality: d(x, z) ≤ d(x, y) + d(y, z).\n\nTopological Spaces (X, τ):\nA set X with collection τ of subsets (\"open sets\") such that:\n- The empty set ∅ and X belong to τ.\n- Any arbitrary union of open sets is open.\n- Any finite intersection of open sets is open.\n\nCore Topological Properties:\n- Compactness: Every open cover of X contains a finite subcover (Heine-Borel: in ℝ^n, a subset is compact if and only if closed and bounded).\n- Connectedness: A space that cannot be partitioned into two disjoint non-empty open sets.",
                  "bodyUrdu": ""
                }
              ],
