                  "bodyEn": "Graph algorithms solve relational connectivity, network flow, and optimal routing problems.\n\nGraph Representation: Adjacency Matrix (O(V²) space, fast edge lookup) vs Adjacency List (O(V + E) space, optimal for sparse networks).\n\nFoundational Graph Algorithms:\n1. Breadth-First Search (BFS): Explores nodes layer-by-layer using a Queue; finds unweighted shortest paths in O(V + E).\n2. Depth-First Search (DFS): Explores branches deeply using Recursion/Stack; detects cycles, finds connected components, and computes Topological Sorting in Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs).\n3. Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm: Computes single-source shortest paths on non-negative weighted graphs using a Min-Heap priority queue in O((V + E) log V).\n4. Minimum Spanning Trees (MST):\n   - Kruskal's Algorithm: Greedily adds smallest edges using a Disjoint-Set (Union-Find) data structure.\n   - Prim's Algorithm: Grows a single tree outward from an initial node using a priority queue.",
