              "sections": [
                {
                  "heading": "Academic Lecture Notes & Analytical Framework",
                  "urduHeading": "علمی نوٹس اور فکری جائزہ",
                  "bodyEn": "Consumer choice theory models how rational economic agents allocate scarce income across competing consumer goods to maximize subjective utility.\n\nIndifference Curve Analysis:\nAn Indifference Curve represents bundles of goods that yield identical levels of total utility to the consumer:\n- Negative Slope: Reflects trade-offs between two goods.\n- Convex to the Origin: Reflects the Diminishing Marginal Rate of Substitution (MRS = -MUx / MUy).\n- Non-Intersecting: Guaranteed by the transitivity axiom of rational preferences.\n\nThe Budget Constraint:\nPx * X + Py * Y = I (Income)\nThe slope of the budget line is determined by the relative price ratio (-Px / Py).\n\nConsumer Equilibrium:\nOccurs at the point of tangency between the budget line and the highest attainable indifference curve:\nMRS_xy = Px / Py  (or MUx / Px = MUy / Py)\n\nSlutsky Decomposition:\nWhen price shifts, total demand response breaks into:\n1. Substitution Effect: Relative price change drives consumers toward the cheaper alternative.\n2. Income Effect: Change in real purchasing power alters total consumption.",
                  "bodyUrdu": ""
                }
              ],
