              "sections": [
                {
                  "heading": "Academic Lecture Notes & Analytical Framework",
                  "urduHeading": "علمی نوٹس اور فکری جائزہ",
                  "bodyEn": "Fiscal policy involves government taxation and expenditure decisions to stabilize economic cycles.\n\nThe Keynesian Multiplier Architecture:\nA discretionary injection of government spending initiates a cumulative economic chain reaction:\n- Marginal Propensity to Consume (MPC): The fraction of an additional dollar of disposable income that a household spends: MPC = ΔC / ΔY.\n- Simple Spending Multiplier: k = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS.\n- Example: If MPC = 0.8, the multiplier is 1 / 0.2 = 5. A $10 billion government expenditure generates $50 billion in aggregate demand expansion.\n\nFiscal Policy Dilemmas:\n1. Crowding-Out Effect: Large government borrowing drives up domestic interest rates, depressing private corporate investment and household borrowing.\n2. Sovereign Debt Traps: When sovereign debt-to-GDP ratios become unsustainable, forcing austerity measures or debt restructuring.",
                  "bodyUrdu": ""
                }
              ],
