                  "bodyEn": "Biomolecules are complex organic macromolecules that execute the metabolic and structural functions of living cells.\n\nMajor Classes of Biomolecules:\n1. Carbohydrates: General formula (CH2O)_n. Monosaccharides (glucose, fructose) link via Glycosidic Bonds to form structural polysaccharides (Cellulose in plants, Chitin) or energy storage reserves (Glycogen, Starch).\n2. Lipids: Hydrophobic macromolecules; Triglycerides (glycerol + 3 fatty acids), Phospholipids, and Steroid hormones. Fatty acids may be saturated (no double bonds; solid at room temperature) or unsaturated (cis double bonds inducing kinks; liquid oils).\n3. Proteins: Linear polymers of 20 standard L-alpha Amino Acids joined via Peptide Bonds:\n   - Primary Structure: Covalent sequence of amino acids.\n   - Secondary Structure: Local hydrogen bonding forming Alpha-Helices and Beta-Pleated Sheets.\n   - Tertiary Structure: 3D globular folding driven by hydrophobic interactions, disulfide bridges, and ionic salt bridges.\n   - Quaternary Structure: Multi-subunit complexes (e.g., tetrameric hemoglobin).",
