              "sections": [
                {
                  "heading": "Academic Lecture Notes & Analytical Framework",
                  "urduHeading": "علمی نوٹس اور فکری جائزہ",
                  "bodyEn": "Phonetics examines physical human speech sounds, while phonology analyzes how sounds function systematically within a language.\n\nThe International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA):\nSolves the inconsistency of English orthography by providing a strict one-to-one mapping between phonetic symbols and distinct speech sounds.\n\nConsonantal Articulation Parameters:\n1. Place of Articulation: Anatomical location of airflow constriction (Bilabial [p, b], Labiodental [f, v], Dental [θ, ð], Alveolar [t, d, s], Velar [k, g], Glottal [h]).\n2. Manner of Articulation: How the vocal tract constricts airflow:\n   - Stops / Plosives: Complete momentary blockage followed by release ([p, b, t, d, k, g]).\n   - Fricatives: Continuous turbulent airflow through a narrow aperture ([f, v, s, z, ʃ, ʒ]).\n   - Nasals: Lowered velum allowing sound to resonate through the nasal cavity ([m, n, ŋ]).\n3. Voicing: Whether the vocal cords vibrate (Voiced [b, d, g, z]) or remain relaxed (Voiceless [p, t, k, s]).",
                  "bodyUrdu": ""
                }
              ],
