                  "bodyEn": "Flourishing along the Indus river basin around 2600–1900 BCE, the Indus Valley (Harappan) Civilization was one of humanity's earliest and most advanced urban societies.\n\nUrban Architectural Marvels:\n1. Standardized Metrology & Brickmaking: All excavated urban centers (Mohenjo-Daro, Harappa, Dholavira, Lothal) utilized baked bricks manufactured to an exact 1:2:4 ratio, alongside precise decimal measurement weights.\n2. Advanced Municipal Sanitation: Two-story homes possessed private bathing rooms draining into covered street sewers equipped with inspection sumps for desiltation—a civic standard unmatched until ancient Rome.\n3. The Great Bath of Mohenjo-Daro: A monumental watertight public basin sealed with natural bitumen (bituminous pitch), indicating ritual ablution practices.\n4. Social Organization & Trade: Lacking royal palaces, monumental monarchical statues, or military garrisons, archaeological evidence points to an egalitarian merchant oligarchy trading lapis lazuli and carnelian with ancient Mesopotamia (Meluhha).",
