                  "bodyEn": "Spanning 1206 to 1526, the Delhi Sultanate established centralized Islamic rule across northern India, repelling the devastating Mongol invasions.\n\nChronology of the Five Dynasties:\n1. Mamluk (Slave) Dynasty (1206–1290): Founded by Qutb-ud-din Aibak; construction of the Qutb Minar. Razia Sultana reigned as the first female monarch of Islamic South Asia.\n2. Khalji Dynasty (1290–1320): Alauddin Khalji implemented draconian market price controls, a standing imperial army, and successfully repulsed repeated massive Mongol invasions.\n3. Tughlaq Dynasty (1320–1414): Muhammad bin Tughlaq pioneered visionary yet catastrophic experiments, including shifting the capital to Daulatabad and issuing token copper currency.\n4. Sayyid & Lodi Dynasties: The final Afghan dynasty, concluded when Ibrahim Lodi fell to Babur at the First Battle of Panipat (1526).\n\nIndo-Islamic Cultural Synthesis:\nFostered the emergence of Hindustani music (Amir Khusrau inventing the Sitar and Qawwali), Indo-Persian literature, and early Urdu.",
