                  "bodyEn": "The uprising of 1857 (termed the \"Sepoy Mutiny\" by colonial historians and the \"War of Independence\" by South Asians) was a mass civil-military rebellion against British exploitation.\n\nCatalysts of the Uprising:\n1. Military & Religious Provocation: Introduction of the Enfield rifle cartridge lubricated with beef and pork fat, which had to be bitten open, violating both Hindu and Muslim religious prohibitions.\n2. Political Dispossession: The arbitrary annexation of Awadh and humiliation of the Mughal royal family.\n3. Economic Ruin: Oppressive land tax settlements driving millions of peasants into perpetual debt.\n\nThe Outbreak & Aftermath:\n- The mutiny erupted at Meerut on May 10, 1857; sepoys marched to Delhi, proclaiming the elderly Bahadur Shah Zafar as Emperor of Hindustan.\n- Brutal suppression concluded with Zafar's exile to Rangoon and the execution of the Mughal princes.\n- Government of India Act 1858: Abolished the East India Company; Queen Victoria assumed direct sovereign governance (The British Raj).",
