                  "bodyEn": "The opening decade of the 20th century witnessed the formal political mobilization of South Asian Muslims.\n\nThe Partition of Bengal (1905):\nViceroy Lord Curzon divided Bengal on administrative grounds, creating a Muslim-majority Eastern Bengal and Assam:\n- Gave Bengali Muslims administrative empowerment, a capital in Dacca, and relief from Calcutta landlords.\n- Met with fierce militant Hindu agitation (Swadeshi boycott movement), resulting in its bitter annulment in 1911.\n\nThe Shimla Deputation & Foundation of the All-India Muslim League:\n- In October 1906, 35 prominent Muslim leaders led by Sir Aga Khan met Viceroy Lord Minto at Shimla, securing Separate Electorates (formally codified in the Morley-Minto Reforms of 1909).\n- On December 30, 1906, during the annual Muhammadan Educational Conference in Dacca, Nawab Salimullah Khan and Nawab Waqar-ul-Mulk founded the All-India Muslim League to champion Muslim civil and political rights.",
