                  "bodyEn": "Muhammad Ali Jinnah (1876–1948) stands as one of the towering constitutional and political figures of the 20th century.\n\nThe Journey from Nationalist to Founding Father:\n1. \"Ambassador of Hindu-Muslim Unity\": Jinnah engineered the historic Lucknow Pact (1916), uniting the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League on a shared constitutional demand for provincial autonomy and separate electorates.\n2. The Nehru Report (1928): Dominated by Motilal Nehru, it repudiated separate electorates and rejected Muslim constitutional safeguards, signaling Congress's unwillingness to share power.\n3. Jinnah's Fourteen Points (1929): Jinnah's definitive constitutional response, demanding a federal system with residual powers to provinces, one-third Muslim representation in the central legislature, and separation of Sindh from Bombay.\n4. \"The Parting of Ways\": Congress's rejection of these points convinced Jinnah that Muslim political survival required separate sovereignty.",
