              "sections": [
                {
                  "heading": "Academic Lecture Notes & Analytical Framework",
                  "urduHeading": "علمی نوٹس اور فکری جائزہ",
                  "bodyEn": "Glaciers are massive rivers of compacted ice that deform under their own weight, acting as immense agents of erosion and deposition.\n\nMechanisms of Glacial Erosion:\n1. Plucking (Quarrying): Meltwater penetrates rock fractures, freezes, and tears shattered blocks of bedrock into the moving ice mass.\n2. Abrasion: Rocks embedded in the glacial sole act like heavy sandpaper, gouging glacial striations and polishing bedrock.\n\nDiagnostic Erosional Landforms:\n- Cirque (Corrie): Steep-sided, armchair-shaped hollow excavated at the head of a mountain valley.\n- Arête & Horn: Knife-edged jagged ridges and pyramidal peaks formed when multiple cirques erode a mountain summit (e.g., the Matterhorn; K2).\n- U-Shaped Glacial Trough: Glaciers widen, deepen, and straighten V-shaped river valleys into steep-sided, flat-bottomed U-shaped valleys with hanging valleys.\n\nMoraines & Glacial Retreat in Pakistan:\nNorthern Pakistan contains the highest concentration of glacial ice outside polar regions (Siachen, Baltoro, Biafo glaciers), vulnerable to climate-induced Glacial Lake Outburst Floods (GLOFs).",
                  "bodyUrdu": ""
                }
              ],
