                  "bodyEn": "Sociology analyzes race and ethnicity as powerful social constructs that structure systemic privilege and exclusion.\n\nConceptual Distinctions:\n- Race: A socially constructed categorization based on perceived physical phenotypes (skin color, facial features), carrying no genetic biological validity.\n- Ethnicity: A shared cultural heritage, language, ancestral homeland, religion, and collective memory.\n\nIntergroup Dynamics:\n1. Prejudice vs Discrimination: Prejudice is a rigid, biased internal attitude; Discrimination is the unequal behavioral action or systemic practice.\n2. Institutional Racism: Systemic policies and institutional practices that produce racially inequitable outcomes independently of individual malice (housing redlining, algorithmic bias).\n3. Pluralism vs Assimilation: Assimilation demands minority groups abandon native cultural heritage to blend into the dominant culture; Pluralism (Multiculturalism) celebrates diverse cultural coexistence.",
